# Writer-starvation soak evidence (2026-09-28, shared local rig)
#
#   scripts/soak_starvation.py --blocks 6 --seconds 90 --readers 10 --writers 2
#
# Question (scale proposal Phase 2): does a continuous read flood on ONE
# database delay a writer enough to justify a writer-preferring bound in
# Store::acquire_queue?  Answer: YES -- measured below, and the bound is now
# built. The queue is a fair, phase-fair, writer-preferring lock.
#
#   verdict: STARVED before the fix, NO_STARVATION after it.
#
# Why docs/soak-2h-evidence.log cannot answer this: that run round-robins
# across four databases, and acquire_queue takes a PER-DATABASE guard, so the
# contended lock is never actually contended. 1.28M requests and 0 errors
# proves nothing about starvation. This harness inverts it: every reader and
# writer hammers ONE hot database. An idle `cold` database is created and
# reported alongside it, so the per-database counters are visibly
# per-database rather than global.
#
# Why the arms are DIFFERENTIAL and INTERLEAVED: this rig is shared and its
# load moved between 27 and 127 over the day, so an absolute latency threshold
# would have measured the neighbours. Blocks alternate
# control/flood/control/flood/... at equal duration, so load drift lands in
# both arms and only the read flood separates them. Every block records the
# machine load it ran at. The verdict is the writer REJECTION RATE, not p99:
# at load ~70 both arms sit on the 500ms ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT ceiling where p99
# saturates and stops discriminating (one run gave 502ms control vs 567ms
# flood, a useless 1.1x, while the rejection rate told the real story).
#
# Attribution is checkable, not assumed: a queue timeout also lands in the
# global requests.capacity_exceeded, so the summary reports the lane-shed
# remainder. It is 0 in every run here, which proves the rejections being
# measured are per-database queue timeouts and not global-lane shedding.
# --writers stays at 2, inside WRITE_PERMITS=4, so no writer is lane-shed.

## summary across the three queue designs

| run | verdict | ctrl rej% | flood rej% | ratio | ctrl w/s | flood w/s | TO ctrl | TO flood | reader 429 flood |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Run 1 | **STARVED** | 0.090 | 0.700 | 7.8 | 33.5 | 14.0 | 8 | 90 | 65 |
| Run 2 | **MARGINAL** | 0.061 | 0.075 | 1.2 | 37.2 | 35.0 | 6 | 4267 | 4260 |
| Run 3 | **NO_STARVATION** | 0.335 | 0.066 | 0.2 | 33.8 | 23.0 | 30 | 44 | 40 |

Machine load per run (mean control / mean flood): Run 1 68.7 / 66.7; Run 2 48.7 / 58.2; Run 3 45.7 / 46.7.
The shipped run met a quieter machine than the pre-fix run, so the decisive
evidence is the WITHIN-RUN differential, which is load-immune by construction,
not the absolute counts.

