## verdict

The pre-fix queue starved writers, and it is not machine noise. The writer
rejection rate is 7.8x higher under the read flood and 3/3 matched block
pairs separate. The pairs are NOT load-matched -- they differ by up to 27%
(59.23/50.61, 66.47/84.73, 80.24/64.73) -- and that cuts the other way, which
is the useful part: in 2 of the 3 pairs the FLOOD block ran on a quieter
machine than its own control and still lost (6 timeouts against 0, and 29
against 0). Losing on a lighter machine is not what a loaded neighbour looks
like. That is the measurement the `acquire_queue` comment was deferring a
decision on, and it says STARVED.

A writer-preferring FLAG over the same RwLock is not an acceptable answer even
though it fixes the writers: it moved the starvation to the readers -- 4,260
reader rejections against 5 writer rejections, 47x more total timeouts. The
shipped queue is a fair phase lock, and it is the only design of the three
whose TOTAL rejection count under the flood is lower than the original's:

| run | writer rejections in the flood | reader rejections | total |
|---|---|---|---|
| Run 1 | 26 (0.700%) | 65 | 91 |
| Run 2 | 7 (0.075%) | 4260 | 4267 |
| Run 3 | 4 (0.066%) | 40 | 44 |

After the fix the flood arm's writer rejection rate (0.066%) is BELOW the
control arm's (0.335%): the read flood no longer hurts writers at all, which
is why the verdict is NO_STARVATION and `all_pairs_separate` is false. Write
throughput under the flood is still 32% below control (23.0/s vs 33.8/s) and
writer p50 is 74.8ms vs 45.6ms, which is the correct outcome for a fair queue
sharing one database: writers wait out the current reader batch instead of
being rejected. Bounded wait, not starvation.

Two honest caveats. The control arm's own rejection rate rose from 0.090% to
0.335% between runs (8 -> 30 timeouts out of ~9,000 writes); both are tiny and
the shipped run met a quieter machine, so run-to-run variance at this load is
the likely cause, but that comparison is not controlled. And no run reached an
idle machine -- the lowest sampled load1 here is 27 on 16 cores. The verdict
rests on the within-run differential precisely because of that.

## reproducing

```
# gated: refuses to report rather than measuring a busy machine
scripts/soak_starvation.py --blocks 6 --seconds 90 --readers 10 --writers 2 \
    --max-load1 4.0 --load-patience 6 --out /tmp/starvation.log
```

Exit 0 = NO_STARVATION, 1 = STARVED/MARGINAL (a real answer: build the bound),
2 = harness failure. The harness aborts rather than reports when the control
arm rejects more than 2% of its writes with no read flood, because that means
the harness itself is the confound -- which is exactly what happened on the
first attempt, when every writer carried a unique Idempotency-Key and
exhausted _dg_replay's MAX_REPLAYS=256 per-principal guard, so the harness
rejected its own writes and reported that as starvation.
